== 0.11 verification that T1 for the M=3 window holds exactly at e_{3+h}=1 and
        necessarily fails at e_{3+h}=0 (where the true transition is T0=identity) ==
   M=3,E=1 : at e_{3+h}=1 T1 exact 58/58 ; at e_{3+h}=0 T1 exact 0/41 (T0 regime)
   M=3,E=2 : at e_{3+h}=1 T1 exact 58/58 ; at e_{3+h}=0 T1 exact 0/41 (T0 regime)
== 0.12 calibration: are gaps {5,7,12} evidence of anything? ==
   Sturmian return times of the factor '11' (slope beta=log2(3/2)):
   gap multiset over 4000 horizons: {5: 379, 7: 300}
   convergent denominators of beta=[0;1,1,2,2,3,1,5,2,23,...]: 1,2,5,12,41,53,...
   -> by the three-distance theorem the return times of ANY Sturmian factor take
      at most 3 values, which are automatically of the form q_k, q_k+q_{k-1}, q_{k+1}.
   So 'gaps near convergents' is forced for any such set, carrying no information.
== 0.13 chance calibration: P(random small integer is 'near a convergent') ==
   tol=0, integers 1..15 : 60% are 'near a convergent/semiconvergent'
   tol=0, integers 1..30 : 43% are 'near a convergent/semiconvergent'
   tol=1, integers 1..15 : 87% are 'near a convergent/semiconvergent'
   tol=1, integers 1..30 : 80% are 'near a convergent/semiconvergent'
   tol=2, integers 1..15 : 100% are 'near a convergent/semiconvergent'
   tol=2, integers 1..30 : 100% are 'near a convergent/semiconvergent'